# systemd --user template for the willow-mcp manifest-grant apply unit
# (pair b74019ac, amending d5504878; unit-honesty rework pair 6bd11def).
# One act per tick: drain $WILLOW_HOME/manifest_grants/pending/, re-verify
# each request's seal and pre-state fresh, sign and publish through
# manifest_admin.set_permission + publish_signed_pair under
# signed_pair_lock, move to done/ or failed/. Type=oneshot because it
# drains what is pending and exits — the .timer beside it owns the
# cadence, same split as willow-mcp-reloader.
#
# Runs in the BROKER's own --user manager, as the broker's OWN uid — no
# User= line here, and none is possible: systemd.exec is explicit that a
# non-root --user service manager may only run a unit as the identity the
# manager itself is running as ("the only valid setting is the same user
# the user's service manager is running as"). A prior draft's
# "User=" line naming a separate trust-owner identity could therefore
# never start (217/USER) once installed here (unit_install_executor.
# unit_dir() always installs into
# the INSTALLING process's own ~/.config/systemd/user — the broker's).
# The request/apply split (manifest_grant_request never signs or
# publishes; only manifest_grant_apply does) is real and stays — but on
# this box it is an AUDIT-TRAIL split, not a privilege boundary, since both
# halves run as the same uid (manifest_grant_executor module docstring,
# gap 85716b25d9a8). manifest_grant_apply's own euid-owns-apps_root check
# still refuses to run as any uid other than the one that owns apps_root —
# on this box that is the broker itself, so the check passes here and
# would correctly refuse a mismatched identity after a real uid split.
#
# What the pending request's own broker_sig proves, precisely: that SOME
# uid-1000 process holding broker_signing_key wrote it — never "the broker,
# uniquely" (no uid split exists on this box, gap 85716b25d9a8). The actual
# privilege boundary here is Kart's sandbox view, measured from inside it
# (sandbox edit, 2026-09-21): config/verifiers.json, env, and
# manifest_grants/ (broker_signing_key included) are ABSENT from a Kart
# task's $WILLOW_HOME — not merely read-only — while nestor.db is present
# but bound read-only (O_RDWR -> EROFS). A uid split, if the operator ever
# performs one, is what would turn broker_sig into "the broker, and only
# the broker" rather than "some uid-1000 process."
#
# Loki audit 3, finding 3 (superseded by the above): a prior version of
# this template carried no WILLOW_KEYRING and no WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT —
# so get_keyring() returned None (every apply EACCES'd, then EALREADY
# burned the pair on the next request) and a signed seat's manifest could
# never be re-signed (efingerprint_absent).
#
# Fix (row 17 ETEMPLATE, amending the above): baking WILLOW_KEYRING= and
# WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT= as Environment= lines asks render_values() to
# fill them from the INSTALLING process's own os.environ — a plain
# `unit_install_execute` call has neither (WILLOW_KEYRING is in Kart's
# env_deny by design; WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT is never exported into the
# broker's own shell), so both placeholders come back unfillable and the
# install refuses ETEMPLATE before a single line is written. Baking either
# one as a literal value in ~/.config/systemd/user would also be wrong in
# shape: a value frozen at install time goes stale exactly like nestor-ui's
# pre-EnvironmentFile draft did (gap cb6623f63b32, "sticky-CLI-args
# split-brain") and manifest_grant_apply already refuses efingerprint_absent
# at RUN time when the fingerprint is missing — install time is the wrong
# place to also assert it. Modeled on nestor-ui.service.template (#595):
# `EnvironmentFile=@WILLOW_HOME@/env` lets systemd re-read the broker's own
# canonical env file on every start, so WILLOW_KEYRING / WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT
# are always whatever the file currently names — never a value baked at
# some past install. WILLOW_HOME and WILLOW_NESTOR_DB stay as explicit
# Environment= lines (placed AFTER EnvironmentFile, so they win over
# same-named keys the file may also carry): both are read by this apply
# path (paths.willow_home(); seal_handler._nestor_db_path()) and both
# resolve cleanly today, so there is nothing to fix there — only
# WILLOW_KEYRING/WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT were unfillable. WILLOW_STORE_ROOT
# and WILLOW_PG_DB are dropped: manifest_grant_executor.py's apply path
# never reads either (grepped clean), so baking them was dead weight, not
# a requirement — they are named in UnsetEnvironment= below instead, the
# same least-exposure move nestor-ui's own template makes for keys an
# unauthenticated tick has no business holding. UnsetEnvironment= (applied
# by systemd AFTER EnvironmentFile=) strips every other key the env file
# may carry that this apply has no need of — the fleet's provider keys, the
# Postgres/store coordinates named above, and the Nestor product's own
# NESTOR_* keys (NESTOR_SEAL_KEY, NESTOR_REQUIRE_SEAL_KEY, NESTOR_KEYRING,
# NESTOR_DB, NESTOR_PERSONAL_DB, NESTOR_PERSONAL_LEDGER) and
# RATATOSK_GROVE_CHANNEL — none of which this apply path reads (grepped
# clean; the apply's own Nestor db path comes from WILLOW_NESTOR_DB,
# seal_handler.py:59, never NESTOR_DB — that name belongs to the separate
# nestor/nestor-ui product). Keys this apply DOES read and must never be
# unset: WILLOW_HOME, WILLOW_NESTOR_DB, WILLOW_KEYRING,
# WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT, WILLOW_MCP_APPS_ROOT, WILLOW_MCP_PYTHON,
# WILLOW_APP_ID, WILLOW_IN_KART.
#
# WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT must be present in $WILLOW_HOME/env — if it is
# missing, every already-signed seat's manifest refuses efingerprint_absent
# at apply time (manifest_grant_executor.py's _seat_pre_state), never
# silently re-signed unsigned. And because this unit signs from a
# non-interactive systemd --user context with no terminal to prompt on,
# the PGP key WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT names must be passphrase-less, or a
# gpg-agent must already be primed before this unit runs (see
# willow-gpg-unlock.service) — a passphrase prompt here has nowhere to go.
#
# ExecStartPre provisions manifest_grants/{pending,done,failed} so the
# first tick never depends on manifest_grant_request having run first —
# and, running as the broker's own uid in the broker's own $WILLOW_HOME,
# this provisioning step actually succeeds.
[Unit]
Description=willow-mcp manifest-grant — apply sealed permission grants

[Service]
Type=oneshot
EnvironmentFile=@WILLOW_HOME@/env
Environment="WILLOW_HOME=@WILLOW_HOME@"
Environment="WILLOW_NESTOR_DB=@NESTOR_DB@"
UnsetEnvironment=ANTHROPIC_API_KEY CEREBRAS_API_KEY GEMINI_API_KEY GROQ_API_KEY HF_API_KEY NESTOR_DB NESTOR_KEYRING NESTOR_PERSONAL_DB NESTOR_PERSONAL_LEDGER NESTOR_REQUIRE_SEAL_KEY NESTOR_SEAL_KEY OPENROUTER_API_KEY RATATOSK_GROVE_CHANNEL WILLOW_EDGE_GROQ_MODEL WILLOW_EDGE_OLLAMA_MODEL WILLOW_GEMINI_MODEL WILLOW_INFERENCE_PROVIDER WILLOW_OLLAMA_MODEL WILLOW_PG_DB WILLOW_PG_USER WILLOW_STORE_ROOT WILLOW_VAULT_BOX
ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'umask 077; mkdir -p "@WILLOW_HOME@/manifest_grants/pending" "@WILLOW_HOME@/manifest_grants/done" "@WILLOW_HOME@/manifest_grants/failed"'
ExecStart=@PYTHON@ -m willow_mcp manifest-grant apply
NoNewPrivileges=true
PrivateTmp=true
